# __name__

Independent Nest 12/Bun application with these integrations prepared at generation:

| Capability             | Prepared                                                |
| ---------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------- |
| PostgreSQL persistence | <!--#if persistence-->yes<!--#else-->no<!--#endif-->    |
| RabbitMQ messaging     | <!--#if messaging-->yes<!--#else-->no<!--#endif-->      |
| Kong exposure          | <!--#if exposure-->yes<!--#else-->no<!--#endif-->       |

The current `application.json` selects applicable configuration, adapters and readiness probes;
withdrawing exposure in it removes the business REST/GraphQL adapters.
Disabled capabilities have no settings, adapters, background connections or
readiness dependency. Operational HTTP is always present. Add business
behavior under `application/` and `domain/` only when needed; no User/Wallet
implementation, CRUD, SQL, event contract, public route or example assertion is
generated. The private package exports no application implementation.
The integration-specific sections below apply only while that capability is
selected. A delivered artifact carries its selection in `app/application.json`.

## Run

From the workspace root, supply an available HTTP port and the settings of the
enabled capabilities:

```sh
__PREFIX___HTTP_PORT=3010 \
<!--#if persistence-->
  __PREFIX___DB_HOST=127.0.0.1 __PREFIX___DB_PORT=5432 __PREFIX___DB_NAME=__name__ \
  __PREFIX___DB_USERNAME=__role__ __PREFIX___DB_PASSWORD=change-me \
<!--#endif-->
<!--#if messaging-->
  __PREFIX___RABBITMQ_URL=amqp://localhost:5672 \
<!--#endif-->
  bun run nx run __name__:serve
```

Use `__name__:watch` or `__name__:debug` for Bun watch/inspector mode. No dotenv
file is loaded and the app does not prepare or delete infrastructure.
<!--#if persistence-->
`__PREFIX___DB_*` selects this application's own database with the restricted
`__role__` runtime role. Startup neither connects eagerly nor migrates.
<!--#endif-->
<!--#if messaging-->
`__PREFIX___RABBITMQ_QUEUE` defaults to `__name__.commands.v1`. Configure broker
heartbeat in the URL when needed and use a unique broker/queue per environment.
HTTP starts while RabbitMQ is unavailable; broker retries use bounded exponential
delays.
<!--#endif-->

## Probes

`/health/live` reports the process without probing dependencies. `/health/ready`
aggregates each applicable component, also
available as `/health/ready/http`, `/health/ready/database`,
`/health/ready/consumer` and `/health/ready/publisher`. Disabled capabilities
and unused messaging roles report `not_applicable`. `/health/backlog` is not
applicable until implemented. HTTP readiness depends on the lifecycle
<!--#if persistence-->
and the database
<!--#endif-->
but never on the broker. They share the application's HTTP listener; keep them
out of client routes.
<!--#if exposure-->
POST `{ "query": "{ httpReady }" }` to `/graphql` for HTTP readiness.
<!--#endif-->

## Extend

Declare functionality groups and required capabilities in `composition.json`,
then bind each group name to a factory in `composition/app.module.ts`
(`functionality`). Factories return module metadata; the same registrations drive
preflight and runtime composition. An unmet requirement fails before adapters
or environment changes. The generated group list starts empty. Use explicit `@Inject(Token)`, factory `inject` lists,
GraphQL type functions and explicit validation; Bun does not emit design metadata.
<!--#if exposure-->

Return business REST controllers as `controllers` and GraphQL resolvers as
`providers` from an exposure group factory (`"exposure": true` in its registration). Only registered adapters are served; nothing is exposed
automatically. Client routes are configured separately in the gateway.
<!--#else-->

Exposure is disabled: there are no business REST or GraphQL adapters, and no
client routes. Before enabling exposure, implement and register its integration in the composition;
changing the selection alone fails preflight.
<!--#endif-->
<!--#if persistence-->

Inject `DATABASE_POOL` from `database/database.module.ts` into application-owned
persistence adapters. Add owned SQL under `database/migrations/` (see its README);
migrations grant `__role__` exactly what the application needs. Run them
explicitly with `DATABASE_APP=__name__ bun run migration:up` inside a prepared
environment; startup never migrates.
<!--#endif-->
<!--#if messaging-->

Register message handlers in a group factory's `providers`, then return their
injection tokens in that factory's `handlers`. Declare `messaging` and any other
required capability in the group registration. Each AMQP message uses a JSON
`{ "pattern": "your.versioned.pattern", "data": ... }` envelope and nonempty AMQP
`messageId` and `correlationId` properties. The adapter passes those identities
explicitly to the handler. Resolve `undefined` after committing effects. Return
`{ accepted: false, reason: 'invalid-your-payload' }` for permanent validation
rejections before effects; use a stable, non-sensitive reason code. Throw for
transient failures that should be retried.

Invalid envelopes and rejected payloads are retained byte-for-byte in
`<queue>.failed`, with publisher confirmation before ACK. Available identities,
content metadata and headers are preserved independently; `x-failure-reason`
records `invalid-json`, `invalid-envelope`, `missing-identity`, `unknown-pattern`
or the handler's rejection reason. Original expiration is omitted so inspection
evidence survives. The retention log includes the same reason and identities.

The 10-second handling deadline ends the AMQP session without acknowledging
uncertain work. It cannot cancel application effects: the transport waits for
the actual operation to settle before reconnecting or completing `stop()`.
A stuck handler keeps this instance unavailable until it settles or the process
is restarted. Other instances can receive redelivery after connection loss;
commit idempotency claims and effects atomically in the owning database before
resolving. This scaffold does not implement a business publisher or an outbox.
<!--#endif-->

SIGINT/SIGTERM refuses new work and drains accepted requests
<!--#if messaging-->
and deliveries, cancelling consumption and closing AMQP first,
<!--#endif-->
before Nest closes HTTP
<!--#if persistence-->
and the database pool
<!--#endif-->
within the shared 15-second deadline; a timeout exits nonzero.

## Check and distribute

To disable a prepared integration, change its `application.json` flag, run
preflight, and prepare the same development run again before starting it.
Keep its source, owned SQL and `composition.json` registration for reactivation.
Required functionality still fails preflight if its dependency is disabled.
Private operational HTTP remains, with inactive probes marked `not_applicable`.
Never-prepared capabilities require explicit author implementation first.

`distribution.json` selects dependency roots and adapter paths per integration.
Rebuild after every selection change: disabled integration paths and migration
interfaces are omitted from delivery, while checkout source remains available
for the next build. Keep shared code outside those paths and load optional
adapters only from selected factories. Preparing the same development run retains
database credentials, data and accepted broker work across reactivation.

```sh
bun run nx run __name__:lint
bun run nx run __name__:typecheck
bun run lint:boundaries
bun run nx run __name__:test
bun run nx run __name__:test-component
bun run nx run __name__:distribution
```

Unit tests use `tests/unit/` without preload or infrastructure. Live tests use
`tests/component/` in an uncached target; their fixtures must own their resources
and cleanup. Neither directory contains generated tests, so each test command
reports no tests and exits nonzero until meaningful tests are added.

Copy `dist/__name__/` outside the workspace, run `bun run preflight` without
credentials, then run `bun run start` there with
the same environment. The artifact includes Bun TypeScript sources, its
declaration, composition registrations and installed runtime dependencies; no workspace or install is
needed. Build for the deployment OS/architecture using the pinned Bun version.
<!--#if persistence-->
With persistence selected, the artifact also provides `migration:up`, `migration:status` and
`migration:down` for this application's own database; supply
`__PREFIX___DB_MIGRATION_USERNAME`/`_PASSWORD` only to those commands.
<!--#else-->
Persistence is disabled, so the artifact has no database migration interface.
<!--#endif-->
